            <ArticleTitle>Commentary: neurotoxicity of local anesthetics--an issue or a scapegoat?</ArticleTitle>
            <Pagination>
                <MedlinePgn>605-10</MedlinePgn>
            </Pagination>
            <Abstract>
                <AbstractText Label="BACKGROUND AND OBJECTIVES" NlmCategory="OBJECTIVE">To evaluate the etiologies of cauda equina syndrome (CES) and transient radicular irritation (TRI) or transient neurologic symptoms (TNSs) following hyperbaric spinal anesthesia.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="METHODS" NlmCategory="METHODS">A review of recent (since 1991) and prior (since 1941) investigations regarding CES and TRI (TNSs) was conducted.</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="RESULTS" NlmCategory="RESULTS">Recent publications fail to recognize significant prior information regarding CES and TRI (TNSs).</AbstractText>
                <AbstractText Label="CONCLUSIONS" NlmCategory="CONCLUSIONS">Cauda equina syndrome is, in all probability, explainable. Further investigation to pinpoint the etiology of TRI (TNSs) is needed.</AbstractText>
            </Abstract>
